.TH BIN2EXE 1 "2026-10-06" "bin2exe 0.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bin2exe \- wrap a flat DOS image in an MZ header
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bin2exe
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.IR file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bin2exe
builds a DOS MZ executable from a flat image such as the COM file
.B uasm -bin
writes.
With no
.BR \-\-header ,
it writes a 32-byte header that runs the image as a COM program:
CS and SS are
.BR FFF0h ,
IP is
.BR 0100h ,
and SP is
.BR FFFEh .
minalloc is 0 and maxalloc is
.BR FFFFh .
DOS adds those segments to the load segment, which wraps them to the PSP,
so the first image byte is PSP:0100 and the stack is the COM stack.
An image larger than 65280 bytes is refused in this mode.
A 16-byte header
.RI ( e_cparhdr
1) is accepted when
.I e_crlc
is 0 or the relocation table fits in those 16 bytes.
The COM wrap above stays 32 bytes.
.PP
.B \-\-header
copies the MZ header of an existing EXE in front of the flat image and
does not change that header.
Use it after
.B dumpexe \-\-uasm
and
.B uasm \-bin
when the original relocations and SS:SP have to match.
If the flat image is a prefix of that original file, the bytes after the
prefix (a trailing overlay) are appended.
.B \-\-no\-tail
leaves the overlay off and warns when the lengths differ.
A matching prefix appends with no extra warning.
Options and file names may be given in any order.
A directory argument expands to non-hidden
.I *.bin
and
.I *.com
files in that directory only.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the usage text and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.BI \-o " path" "\fR, \fB\-\-output " path
Write one output file, write every output into a directory, or write the
single EXE to standard output when
.I path
is
.BR \- .
The default output is
.I <stem>.exe
beside each input.
An existing default
.I <stem>.exe
is left unchanged; pass
.B \-o
to replace a regular file.
A symbolic link at
.I path
is an error and is not followed.
Several inputs and one existing file name produce
.I <stem>_<input>.exe
and a warning.
A path that does not exist, given for several inputs, is created as a directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-header " exe"
Copy the MZ header from
.I exe
(the first
.I e_cparhdr
paragraphs, including the relocation table) in front of each flat image.
Page counts, relocations, SS:SP, minalloc, and the checksum are not
rewritten.
This mode is off unless the option is given.
An invalid or oversized header is reported against this path, not against
the flat image.
The MZ header itself must be at most 65536 bytes. The rest of the file is
read as well so a tail past that cap can be kept.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-tail
Do not append the original bytes that follow a flat image which is a
prefix of the
.B \-\-header
file.
Tail carry is on unless this option is given.
There is no
.B \-\-tail
switch.
.SH NOTES
A flat image has no entry point. The copied header carries CS:IP.
dumpexe writes a bare
.B end
on a multi-segment listing so
.B uasm \-bin
accepts it.
COM listings keep
.BR "end func_0100" .
.PP
The default wrap asks DOS for the largest free block
(maxalloc FFFFh) so the COM stack at PSP:FFFE is inside the allocation.
Header copy keeps a trailing overlay when the flat image is a prefix of
the original file.
.B \-\-no\-tail
omits that overlay. A length mismatch that did not append a tail is a
warning, and the file is still written.
The MZ header is limited to 65536 bytes. The
.B \-\-header
file is read in full, up to 32 MiB plus that header, so the tail is not
cut at 65536 bytes.
A flat image is limited to 32 MiB, and the default wrap is also limited
to 65280 bytes.
The output is written with
.BR mkstemp (3)
and
.B O_NOFOLLOW
in the destination directory, then renamed into place only after the
write succeeds.
A symlink is not replaced.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, help, or version.
.TP
.B 1
The image could not be read or written, or it is not a usable EXE input.
.TP
.B 2
The command line is wrong.
.SH EXAMPLES
Wrap a COM image:
.RS
.B uasm \-bin \-nologo \-Fo HELLO.COM HELLO.asm
.B bin2exe HELLO.COM
.RE
.PP
Restore an original header after a UASM round trip:
.RS
.B dumpexe \-\-uasm \-o GAME.asm GAME.EXE
.B uasm \-bin \-nologo \-Fo GAME.bin GAME.asm
.B bin2exe \-\-header GAME.EXE GAME.bin \-o GAME.restored.exe
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dumpexe (1)
.SH AUTHORS
bin2exe contributors.
